# Archivers, each one making an archive and then reading it
# back -- a round trip, not a usage line.

shot    ar
cap     Carl Kreider's ar, the OS-9 archive librarian: -u to add, -t for
cap     the table of contents.  Every path it is given must be RELATIVE --
cap     handed an absolute one it answers `absolute path illegal' -- so
cap     these are written from the data directory, which is /dd.
run     rm -f tmp/k2.ar
wait    4
run     ar -u tmp/k2.ar SRC/keep/keep.c
wait    8
run     ar -t tmp/k2.ar
wait    6

shot    ar2
size    36 80
for     ar2 liborder libsplit unpacklib unpacklib.os9
cap     Ar V2.00, a later edition than the V1.2 that ships as `ar', and the
cap     three tools that take an OS-9 module LIBRARY apart -- `libsplit'
cap     into its members, `unpacklib' the same job by another hand, and
cap     `liborder' reporting the order they sit in.  Each is asked for
cap     itself here.
run     ar2
wait    8
run     liborder
wait    8
run     libsplit
wait    8
run     unpacklib
wait    8
run     unpacklib.os9
wait    8

shot    arc
for     arc marc dearc
cap     ARC, the format that came before zip -- Stephan Paschedag's OS-9
cap     port of System Enhancement Associates' 5.21.  `a' adds and `v'
cap     lists with the compression column.  The archive name has to be
cap     RELATIVE: arc builds it under a temporary name and then moves it,
cap     and the move fails on an absolute target -- this C library has no
cap     rename().  `marc' merges two archives; `dearc' reads them.
run     rm -f m.arc
wait    4
run     arc a m.arc /dd/SYS/motd /dd/SYS/termcap
wait    12
run     arc v m.arc
wait    8
run     marc -?
wait    6
run     dearc < /nil
wait    8

shot    cat-combine
size    36 80
for     cat combine demerge modbuster
cap     `combine' interleaves two files BYTE BY BYTE -- even bytes from the
cap     first, odd from the second -- which is how a 16-bit EPROM image is
cap     rebuilt from its two 8-bit halves.  Eight A's and eight B's come
cap     back as ABABABAB, twice over, and that is the whole program.
cap     `demerge' is unrelated despite the names -- it splits a file of
cap     MERGED MODULES into one file per module, named after the module, and
cap     nothing on this disk makes such a file, so its usage is all there is
cap     to show.
run     cat /dd/SYS/motd
wait    6
run     echo AAAAAAAA > /dd/tmp/even
wait    5
run     echo BBBBBBBB > /dd/tmp/odd
wait    5
run     combine /dd/tmp/even /dd/tmp/odd /dd/tmp/mix -q
wait    8
run     cat /dd/tmp/mix
wait    6
run     demerge -?
wait    6

shot    compr
for     compr
cap     compr, an OS-9 file compressor of its own, on the disk's index.
run     cp /dd/DOC/INDEX /dd/tmp/idx3
wait    8
run     compr /dd/tmp/idx3
wait    12
run     ls -l /dd/tmp/idx3*
wait    6

shot    compress
cap     compress, the LZW one, reporting what it saved.
run     cat /dd/DOC/INDEX > /dd/tmp/index.txt
wait    3
run     compress -v /dd/tmp/index.txt
wait    8

shot    gzip
cap     GNU gzip on the same file, for comparison with compress.
run     cat /dd/DOC/INDEX > /dd/tmp/index2.txt
wait    3
run     gzip -v /dd/tmp/index2.txt
wait    8

shot    gzips
for     gzip020_csl gzip020_nocsl gzip68k_csl gzip68k_nocsl compress_4.0
for     gzipcpu32k_csl gzipcpu32_nocsl
cap     Four builds of gzip 1.2.4 -- 68000 and 68020, with and without the
cap     csl trap library -- kept side by side so you can take the one your
cap     machine wants.  Each says which it is.
cap     All four are asked for; two were until 2026-08-30.
run     gzip68k_nocsl -V
wait    8
run     gzip020_nocsl -V
wait    8
run     gzip68k_csl -V
wait    8
run     gzip020_csl -V
wait    8
run     gzipcpu32k_csl -V
wait    8
run     gzipcpu32_nocsl -V
wait    8

shot    lha
cap     LHa 2.08 on the same file: pack, then list with the ratio.
run     lha a /dd/tmp/k.lzh /dd/SRC/keep/keep.c
wait    6
run     lha l /dd/tmp/k.lzh
wait    4

shot    lharc
for     lharc lharcs xlharc
cap     LHarc, the older sibling of lha: pack, then list.
run     lharc a /dd/tmp/l.lzh /dd/SYS/motd
wait    10
run     lharc l /dd/tmp/l.lzh
wait    8
run     xlharc < /nil
wait    8

shot    modbuster
cap     modbuster splits a file of merged OS-9 modules back into separate
cap     ones; unpacklib does the same for a linker library.
run     modbuster -?
wait    4
run     unpacklib -?
wait    4

shot    shar
for     shar
cap     shar wraps files as a shell archive -- how source travelled over
cap     usenet, and how most of this collection arrived.  ONE BROKEN CHECK
cap     stops it: its read-access test rejects every file that EXISTS, and
cap     the file below is world-readable with `cat' reading it happily.
cap     Give it a name that is NOT there and the check passes vacuously --
cap     and out comes the whole preamble, cut line and all.  The archiver
cap     works; the gatekeeper does not.
run     ls -l /dd/readme
wait    5
run     shar readme
wait    6
run     shar not-a-file-here | head -n 9
wait    8

shot    tar
cap     GNU tar 1.10 packing a directory off this disk and listing what it
cap     wrote -- the same tar the image build uses to populate the disk.
run     tar cvf /dd/tmp/keep.tar /dd/SRC/keep
wait    5
run     tar tvf /dd/tmp/keep.tar
wait    4

shot    unzip
size    36 80
for     unzip zipnote zipsplit funzip zipinfo
cap     Info-ZIP's readers, each asked for itself.  `unzip' lists an
cap     archive that `zip' on this disk cannot write -- see the note
cap     against zip -- `zipnote' reads and writes an archive's comments,
cap     `zipsplit' cuts one into pieces that fit a floppy, and `funzip'
cap     unzips a stream.  The last three were credited here and never run.
run     unzip -v
wait    10
run     zipnote
wait    8
run     zipsplit
wait    8
run     funzip
wait    8
run     zipinfo
wait    8

shot    zoo
cap     zoo, packing and then listing -- compression ratio per member.
run     zoo a /dd/tmp/k.zoo /dd/SRC/keep/keep.c
wait    5
run     zoo l /dd/tmp/k.zoo
wait    4

shot    zoo2
for     zoo_2.1 fiz booz
cap     zoo 2.1 writes an archive, booz lists it, and fiz walks its
cap     structure directory by directory. booz takes a BARE LETTER, not a
cap     dash-letter, and fiz takes the archive as an argument.
run     zoo_2.1 a /dd/tmp/z2.zoo /dd/SYS/motd
wait    10
run     booz l /dd/tmp/z2.zoo
wait    8
run     fiz /dd/tmp/z2.zoo
wait    10

shot    zip
cap     Info-ZIP's zip DEFLATES correctly and then cannot rename its
cap     temporary file over the target -- `_Z000003' is left behind and
cap     the archive is never written.  Two directories were tried.  This
cap     is one of the three failures the data tests keep failing on
cap     purpose; `unzip' reads other people's archives perfectly well.
run     mkdir /dd/tmp/zt
wait    6
run     cp /dd/SYS/motd /dd/tmp/zt/motd
wait    6
run     zip /dd/tmp/zt/test.zip /dd/tmp/zt/motd
wait    15
run     dir /dd/tmp/zt
wait    10


shot    alternates
for     diff_1.1 gtar m4_0.5 lharcs compress_4.0 sed_1.06 zoo_2.1
cap     CMDS/REBUILT keeps a second build of several programs because they
cap     do not all behave the same -- here diff 1.1 answers with its whole
cap     option list, which is the difference you would be choosing between.
cap     Each of the seven is asked for its own version.
run     diff_1.1 -v
wait    10
run     gtar --version
wait    10
run     m4_0.5 --version
wait    10
run     lharcs
wait    10
run     compress_4.0 -V
wait    10
run     sed_1.06 --version
wait    10
run     zoo_2.1
wait    10
